## Promotion options

| Criterion | A: direct evaluator validation | B: CAS bundle with revalidation | C: wrapper/package-only checks |
|---|---|---|---|
| Enforcement coverage | Mandatory in existing evaluator/controller chain | Mandatory if evaluator resolves and verifies bundle | Bypassable through direct calls |
| Accuracy/integrity | Exact candidate bytes and stream; origin unresolved | Identical content; CAS proves no origin | Stale or detached report possible |
| Latency | Scan bytes and rows each evaluation | CAS reads plus validation; no proven speed gain | Cheap lookup but insufficient |
| Memory | Bounded candidate processing possible; helper materializes rows | Bundle storage plus decoded rows | Small at promotion; work moved elsewhere |
| Maintainability | Fewest concepts; API composition unresolved | Additional schema, version and anchoring rules | Simple helper, duplicated enforcement expectation |
| Complexity/risk | Moderate; source binding decisive | Higher; storage integrity can resemble authority | Low cost, unacceptable bypass |
| Eligibility | Conditional on genuine producer | Conditional on genuine anchor | Rejected as enforcement |

A is recommended provisionally because it reuses the consequential boundary without another trust object. B remains viable only if existing interfaces expose an anchored CAS representation. Neither is implementation-ready until producer ownership and candidate/run binding are demonstrated within scope. A default-deny argument alone does not complete the invariant.

For Council binding, prefer one canonical serializer shared by recording and verification over duplicated field lists. This reduces omission drift but creates common-mode risk requiring independent field-mutation controls. Preserve vote_cas_hash='' in the hashed representation before attaching its digest. Changing that convention requires compatibility analysis, not historical rewrites.

## Risks for adversarial examination

- R01 CRITICAL — fabricated origin: consistent locally assembled rows or CAS references could impersonate production evidence. Require a demonstrated runtime-owned producer/anchor and candidate/run binding; otherwise hold that design path.
- R02 HIGH — candidate substitution: scanning one snapshot while hashing/promoting another creates a timing gap. Bind scanning to exact candidate bytes and reject changes; challenge symlinks and repository-relative test classification.
- R03 HIGH — false lifecycle success: broad event-name prefixes can count failure or unrelated-run events. Resolve genuine success/identity fields; test failure events and mixed streams without editing protected emitters.
- R04 HIGH — Council omission or schema drift: partial comparisons, non-object payloads, duplicate/mismatched voter entries and missing historical fields can hide dissent. Require complete consistency checks, explicit legacy disposition and one-field negatives.
- R05 HIGH — vacuous verification: an unrelated bootstrap rejection can mask a missing check. Isolate the intended denial and minimally changed valid control through real evaluator/controller functions; no successful stubs or manufactured external observations.
- R06 HIGH — scope/output expansion: tooling may touch historical artifacts or require protected producers. Inspect scoped sources, use permitted disposable outputs, and remand before any undeclared write or Builder expansion.

These hazards require safeguards; none is accepted here.

## Reversibility and stopping boundary

Planning and isolated source changes are reversible. Production ref updates, historical package replacement and consumed-attempt reuse are unauthorized. Denial controls must establish absence of promotion/preparation effects. Preserve automatic-promotion, bootstrap, capability and protected-ref restrictions.

No candidate freeze, v14 package-root update, qualification claim, provider substitution or protected verifier change is permitted. Preserve P1/PRE-P2/P2/DEBT-0250 qualified baselines, P3-P9_BLOCKED and SENTIUM_FROZEN. Immutable authority, find-delete, transcript-provenance and catalog-identity residuals remain separate. L07 should challenge R01-R06 before any option is treated as implementation-ready.
